    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm putting together a hypertrophy program for a client and need to get specific on the nutritional side. Can you explain the mechanistic role that the amino acid Leucine plays in triggering the mTOR pathway? I also want to make sure I'm explaining the relationship between a caloric surplus and nitrogen balance correctly for their meal plan.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Leucine, a crucial branched-chain amino acid, acts as a metabolic switch, directly signaling the mTOR pathway to initiate muscle protein synthesis. It does this by binding to and activating the Rag GTPases, which then recruit mTOR to the lysosomal surface where it can be activated. Essentially, leucine provides the necessary \"on\" signal for the machinery to build new muscle tissue.\n\nFor your client's meal plan, a caloric surplus is vital because it ensures the body has excess energy, which is necessary to fuel the anabolic processes driven by the mTOR pathway. When energy intake exceeds expenditure, the body is in a positive nitrogen balance, meaning it is retaining more nitrogen (from protein) than it is excreting. This surplus of building blocks, combined with the leucine signal, allows for net muscle gain.",
      "source": "target",
